.TH TROFF 1
.SH NAME
troff \- typeset text
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B troff
[
.BI -m x
] [
.BI -r x = y
] [
.BI -d x = y
] [
.B -C
] [
.BI -T dev
] [
.BI -F dir
] [
.BI -M dir
] [
.I file ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Troff
sets the text of its
.I files
(or of its standard input) in type, as their requests and escapes
direct, and writes on its standard output a description of the pages
it made, independent of any device, for
.IR tr2pdf (1)
to make a PDF of, or for the document views
.RI ( wm/page (1),
Xenith, Lucifer) to show as it is.
It is a translation of Ali Gholami Rudi's Neatroff:
its language is Neatroff's, which is troff's with long names
.RB ( \e*[name] ,
.BR \en[name] ),
OpenType's features and kerning, a paragraph filled at once
(as TeX fills one), and hyphenation by TeX's patterns
(English and Ancient Greek here).
For the same input and fonts its output is, byte for byte, Neatroff's.
.PP
Its preprocessors
.IR eqn (1),
.IR tbl (1),
.IR pic (1)
and
.IR refer (1)
set mathematics, tables, pictures and citations;
.IR book (1)
runs them all, and
.I troff
as often as a book's references need.
The macro packages are described in
.IR troff (6).
.PP
The options are:
.TP
.BI -m x
Read the macro package
.BI tmac. x
(from
.BR /lib/tmac )
before the files:
.B -ms
for papers,
.B -man
for manual pages,
.B -mbook
for books.
.TP
.BI -r x = y
Set the number register
.I x
to
.IR y .
.TP
.BI -d x = y
Define the string
.I x
as
.IR y .
.TP
.B -C
Compatibility: names of two characters, run into what follows them,
as old troff reads them.
.TP
.BI -T dev
Set for the device
.I dev
(the default,
.BR utf :
Libertinus, its Serif, Sans, Mono and Math, and DejaVu Sans for what
they lack).
.TP
.BI -F dir
Read the device's description from
.I dir
(default
.BR /lib/troff ).
.TP
.BI -M dir
Read macro packages from
.I dir
(default
.BR /lib/tmac ).
.SS "Not Neatroff's"
These are this troff's; with none of them requested,
it sets as Neatroff sets.
.TP
.BI .mtp " n"
Protrusion: with
.I n
not zero, punctuation, hyphens and some letters at a line's ends hang
into the margins, each by the thousandths of its width
.B .mtpc
gives it, so that the text's edges look straight.
.TP
.BI ".mtpc " "font left right chars ..."
Set the protrusion of
.IR chars ,
in
.IR font ,
at a line's left and its right.
.TP
.BI ".mte " "stretch shrink step"
Expansion: a justified line's glyphs may be widened or narrowed, by up
to
.I stretch
and
.I shrink
thousandths in steps of
.IR step ,
so that its spaces vary less; tr2pdf draws them so.
.TP
.BI .wo " n"
Widows and orphans: with
.I n
not zero, a paragraph is broken by a page only with two of its lines
at least on either side.
.TP
.BI .rules " n"
Rules drawn: with
.I n
not zero (or none), the
.B \el
and
.B \eL
of rule characters
.RB ( \e(ul ,
.BR \e(ru ,
.BR \e(rn ,
.BR \e(br ...)
are drawn, each a rectangle where its glyphs' ink would be, not set as
glyphs side by side.
.TP
.BI .mg " n"
With
.I n
not zero, a character no font has is said on the standard error
(once), not dropped without a word.
.TP
.B \en[.pend]
The lines the text not yet set will take, were the paragraph to end
here.
.TP
.BI \eX'nr " name value" '
Set the register
.I name
to
.I value
as the line it is in goes out (not when it is read): what is set on a
page, known at the page's foot.
.PP
A pair is kerned as its font kerns it, once: a font's kerning lookup
that its scripts share is performed once, not once for each script.
.SH EXAMPLES
A paper, as a PDF:
.IP
.EX
troff -ms paper.ms | tr2pdf >paper.pdf
.EE
.PP
Its tables and equations first:
.IP
.EX
tbl paper.ms | eqn | troff -ms | tr2pdf >paper.pdf
.EE
.SH FILES
.TF /lib/troff/devutf
.TP
.B /lib/troff/devutf
the device's description and its fonts', made by
.IR mkfn (1)
.TP
.B /lib/tmac
the macro packages
.TP
.B /fonts/libertinus
the fonts
